• T2 is complete and verified. What was built, all inside `frontend/src/data/`:

  - **`types.ts`** — `Device`, `Reading`, `Site`, `DeviceStatus`, `DeviceKind`, and per-kind metric shapes (camera: uptime/bitrate; switch: load amps; fridge: temperature; thermostat: setpoint vs actual; intercom: calls/signal).
  - **`prng.ts`** — seeded `mulberry32` plus sampling helpers; no `Math.random` anywhere (grep-verified).
  - **`fleet.ts`** — `generateFleet()`: 240 devices across 6 named sites and 5 kinds, realistic per-kind reporting intervals, 24h of history at 5-minute resolution with mean-reverting drift and daily cycles. Health mix leaves ~10 critical, ~7 serious, ~10 offline, plus flaky and "recovered" devices so the dashboard has something to show. Same seed ⇒ byte-identical fleet.
  - **`status.ts`** — pure `deriveStatus(device, reading, now)`: stale past the expected interval ⇒ `offline`, otherwise kind-specific thresholds. Status is never stored or assigned.
  - **`useLiveFeed.ts`** — advances the simulation every 2s (30 sim-seconds), exposes `{ fleet, lastUpdated, paused, setPaused }`, clears its interval on unmount.
  - **`queries.ts`** — exactly 8 pre-defined queries as pure `{ id, label, description, predicate }` objects.
  - **`index.ts`** — barrel export.

  Two bugs were caught by a behavioural smoke test (not just the build): sim state was keyed by device object identity, so everything silently went offline after the first live tick — re-keyed by device id; and the "recovered in the last hour" query's semantics didn't match the generator's recovery window — aligned them.

